<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management: the single place a package version is declared
    (ADR-0026 section C, "Central Package Management is the single place that
    declares versions, which makes scanning and pinning straightforward").

    It lands with the first package rather than with the first project, and that
    was deliberate. `ManagePackageVersionsCentrally` over zero packages proves
    nothing at all: nothing can violate it, so nothing can demonstrate it works.
    With one package it is measurable, and it was measured on 2026-08-02:
    putting `Version="5.6.0"` back on the PackageReference fails the build with
    NU1008, and deleting the PackageVersion line below fails it with NU1010.
    Two different errors for two different mistakes, both exit 1.

    This file is also the answer to an item two documents have been carrying.
    ADR-0061's maintenance rule and `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md` section 7 both
    say the durable completeness check is to reconcile their prose lists against
    the real dependency manifest "once code exists at M1", because two lists
    derived from prose can agree with each other while both omit the same thing.
    That manifest is this file. It is one row long, so the check is not worth
    automating yet, but it is no longer blocked, and it should be automated
    before the row count makes a manual read unreliable.

    A VERSION WRITTEN LIKE THE ONE BELOW IS A FLOOR, NOT A PIN, and the two are
    easy to confuse in exactly the file whose job is pinning. Read out of
    `obj/project.assets.json` after restore on 2026-08-02, this declaration
    produces the constraint `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers >= 5.6.0`
    and resolves to `5.6.0`, because NuGet takes the lowest version satisfying a
    direct constraint. The resolution is what a reader sees and the constraint
    is what is actually written, so a yanked or unlisted 5.6.0 would float this
    forward silently. Exact pinning is the bracket form, `[5.6.0]`.

    ADR-0021 parameter A settled the OpenIddict half of that on 2026-08-02:
    "exactly" is the bracket form, it is required of OpenIddict and its
    sub-packages, and it is deliberately NOT imposed on every row here, because
    a package with a deep graph can pay a restore conflict no decision asked
    for. So the row below stays plain and the engine's rows will not, and that
    difference is a decision rather than an inconsistency.

    What a bracket still does not buy is a reproducible graph. It bounds the
    direct constraint and nothing beneath it, so transitive versions remain
    whatever the graph allows on the day of the restore, which reaches the
    ADR-0026 licence scan as much as it reaches the pin. That gap is named in
    the same parameter and was decided on 2026-08-03 by ADR-0095, which
    DECLINES a committed lock file: NuGet reproduces a closure unless one of
    three documented cases applies, and none is reachable while every row here
    is fixed and nuget.org is the only source. Two of those three conditions
    are now rules rather than circumstances, enforced by Check 9 of the docs
    guardrail, so a wildcard in this file or a tracked NuGet.config fails the
    build. What the decline gives up is content-hash verification of package
    content and a reviewable diff of transitive change, both recorded there as
    accepted risk and carried to the pre-GA checklist.

    ONE SENTENCE BELOW IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and ADR-0095 parameter D.3 says so
    rather than repeating it. The claim that an unlisted 5.6.0 would float this
    forward silently is this file's own; the source read for that ADR covers a
    package version being REMOVED from a repository, which nuget.org does not
    permit, and says nothing about a version that is unlisted there and still
    restorable by exact reference. Treat it as an open question, not a fact.

    THE RESTORE GRAPH STOPPED BEING TRIVIAL ON 2026-08-02. With only the
    analyzer row it was one node deep, the analyzer bringing no transitive
    packages at all, and that reading is kept because it is what made the
    following one worth taking. Adding the two test rows below took the
    architecture-test project's graph to TWENTY-THREE packages plus its one
    project reference, read from its `obj/project.assets.json`, out of two
    direct package references.

    That matters to the ADR-0026 licence-scan gate in both directions. A green
    scan over a one-node graph said almost nothing about the scanner; a graph
    this shape is the first thing here that could actually exercise one. And it
    is the first place where "read the licence of what is distributed" costs
    more than three reads, so section 3 of `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md`
    enumerates all twenty-three rather than only the two that are written here.

    One node in it is worth naming: `Microsoft.ApplicationInsights`, pulled in
    by `Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.Telemetry`, which the testing platform
    auto-registers. It is test-only and is never packed into a shipped
    artifact, and the CI job opts out by environment variable rather than
    leaving the default. No ADR rules on transmitting build telemetry, so that
    is a choice made in the workflow and stated there, not a decision cited
    from somewhere.

    Licence, before adoption rather than after (ADR-0026, and section 7 of the
    licence record: "read the licence text of the thing that is actually
    distributed"). Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers 5.6.0 is MIT, read
    on 2026-08-02 in the package's own .nuspec fetched from the nuget.org flat
    container, which carries `<license type="expression">MIT</license>`. Two
    documents in this repository already asserted MIT for it in prose, which is
    blind spot 2 of the licence record and is not evidence. Recorded with its
    source in `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md` section 3.

    The two test rows were read the same way on 2026-08-02: xunit.v3 3.2.2 and
    TngTech.ArchUnitNET.xUnitV3 0.13.3 both carry
    `<license type="expression">Apache-2.0</license>` in their own .nuspec.

    THE xUnit INTEGRATION PACKAGE NAME IS NOT COSMETIC. The plainly named
    `TngTech.ArchUnitNET.xUnit` at the same version declares a dependency on
    `xunit.assert 2.4.1`, which is xUnit v2, while ADR-0060 binds tests to what
    `xunit.v3.assert` ships. The `xUnitV3` variant declares `xunit.v3.assert`
    instead and is the one this project takes. Both nuspecs were read; the
    documents that name this library (ADR-0024, design 01 and design 20) all
    write the base name `TngTech.ArchUnitNET` and none of them chooses between
    the integration variants, so the choice is made here.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" Version="5.6.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="TngTech.ArchUnitNET.xUnitV3" Version="0.13.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
